<main>
<section id="brief">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
    <span class="label">THE BRIEF</span>
  <h2>Take something familiar. Question everything about it.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">Redefine is a 24-hour designathon where creativity meets innovation, challenging participants to rethink the digital experiences they interact with every day.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="body-copy">
    <p>Rather than simply improving existing platforms, participants are encouraged to <strong>question established design patterns, challenge assumptions, and reimagine how technology can serve users in entirely new ways.</strong></p>
    <p>From reinventing Spotify and transforming e-commerce to redesigning social platforms, productivity tools, entertainment experiences, or everyday applications, the possibilities are limitless. The goal is not to build another version of what already exists, but to create something bold, meaningful, and distinctly different.</p>
    <p>Throughout the 24-hour challenge, participants will explore user experience, visual design, interaction design, and emerging technologies to turn unconventional ideas into compelling digital experiences. They will identify limitations in familiar platforms, rethink their core interactions, and develop solutions that are intuitive, engaging, accessible, and future-ready.</p>
    <p>Redefine is ultimately about breaking away from the expected. It provides a space for designers, developers, innovators, and creative thinkers to experiment beyond conventional boundaries and transform ambitious ideas into tangible experiences.</p>
    <p>At Redefine, there are no fixed answers. The challenge is simple: <strong>take something familiar, question everything about it, and build something better.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><b>24</b><span>Hours, no pause</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>1–4</b><span>Members per team</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>₹150</b><span>Per ticket</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>5 figures</b><span>Prize pool, dropping soon</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="tracks">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
    <span class="label">TRACKS</span>
  <h2>What would you redefine?</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">A set of tracks is released when the brief drops on the morning of the 16th, and every team picks one to work inside. The categories below are the kind of ground they’ll cover.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards" id="trackGrid"></div>
</section>

<section id="timeline">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
    <span class="label">TIMELINE</span>
  <h2>Twenty-four hours, one clock</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">Doors on the morning of 16 September at Sarojini Naidu Gallery; submissions close the following morning. Exact times go out to registered teams.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-row"><div class="tl-when">16 SEP · Early morning</div><div class="tl-what">Check-in <span class="tba">TBA</span></div><p class="tl-note">Reporting time before the opening bell goes out to registered teams.</p></div>
    <div class="tl-row pin"><div class="tl-when">16 SEP · Morning</div><div class="tl-what">Brief drops · clock starts</div><p class="tl-note">The problem statement and judging rubric go out. Twenty-four hours on the clock from here.</p></div>
    <div class="tl-row"><div class="tl-when">16 SEP · Afternoon</div><div class="tl-what">Checkpoint review <span class="tba">TBA</span></div><p class="tl-note">Progress reviews during the run. Format and timing to be announced.</p></div>
    <div class="tl-row"><div class="tl-when">16 SEP · Night</div><div class="tl-what">Overnight session</div><p class="tl-note">The gallery stays open through the night. The clock doesn’t stop.</p></div>
    <div class="tl-row pin"><div class="tl-when">17 SEP · Morning</div><div class="tl-what">Submissions close</div><p class="tl-note">Twenty-four hours up. Final files and prototypes in.</p></div>
    <div class="tl-row"><div class="tl-when">17 SEP · Later</div><div class="tl-what">Judging &amp; results <span class="tba">TBA</span></div><p class="tl-note">Scored on architecture, colour palette, typography and creativity.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="judging">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
    <span class="label">JUDGING</span>
  <h2>Four things the judges score</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">Craft carries as much weight as the idea. A radical concept in careless type and colour won’t clear the bar — and neither will a beautiful screen that changes nothing.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Criterion 01</span><h3>Architecture</h3><p>How the experience is structured — information hierarchy, navigation, and whether the flow holds up under real use.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Criterion 02</span><h3>Colour Palette</h3><p>Deliberate colour with a working system behind it: contrast, accessibility, and a palette chosen for this product.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Criterion 03</span><h3>Typography</h3><p>Type as a decision, not a default. Scale, pairing, weight, spacing, and readability across the whole interface.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Criterion 04</span><h3>Creativity</h3><p>How far you moved from the original. The bolder the departure — and the better you justify it — the higher this scores.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="teams">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
    <span class="label">TEAM UP</span>
  <h2>Teams are one to four</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">Enter solo or bring up to three others. Everyone on the team registers individually, and you name your team when you do — put the same team name on every entry so we can group you.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Solo</span><h3>1 member</h3><p>A valid entry. You compete on the same criteria as everyone else, on the same clock.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Small</span><h3>2–3 members</h3><p>The common shape. Enough to split research, visual design and prototyping without losing time to coordination.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="card-k">Full</span><h3>4 members</h3><p>The maximum. Worth it if you can genuinely parallelise — twenty-four hours punishes teams that can’t.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="prize">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
      <span class="label">PRIZE POOL</span>
      <h2>Five digits. Number drops soon.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">The pool is locked in and it runs to five figures. The exact amount — and how it splits across the winning teams — is announced closer to the event.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prize-band">
    <div class="prize-figure" aria-label="A five-digit rupee prize pool, yet to be announced">₹ _ _ _ _ _</div>
    <div><p>Scored on the same four criteria as everything else: architecture, colour palette, typography and creativity.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="faq">
  <div class="head">
    <div>
      <span class="label">FAQ</span>
      <h2>Before you ask</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">Anything not covered here goes out to registered teams by email before the 16th.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <details open><summary>What does it cost?</summary><p>₹150 per ticket. That’s per person, and it covers the full 24 hours at the venue.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I enter on my own?</summary><p>Yes. Teams are 1 to 4 members, so a solo entry is a valid team.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is this a coding event?</summary><p>It’s a designathon. The judging criteria are architecture, colour palette, typography and creativity — so the work is judged as design. How far you take it into a working prototype is up to you and your team.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What will I be designing?</summary><p>A set of tracks is announced at the start of the event, and your team chooses one of them to work in. Expect familiar territory — music and streaming, e-commerce, social platforms, productivity tools, entertainment, everyday apps. Whichever track you pick, the rule is the same: you’re replacing something familiar, not polishing it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I start designing before the event?</summary><p>The brief drops at 08:00 on 16 September and the clock starts there. Coming in with sharpened tools, a set-up file and a point of view is fine; coming in with the deliverable is not.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where exactly is it?</summary><p>Sarojini Naidu Gallery, from 08:00 AM on 16 September to 08:00 AM on 17 September. Reporting time and what to bring will be sent to registered teams.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="final">
  <span class="label">GET IN</span>
  <h2>REDEFINE</h2>
  <p class="sub">16–17 September · 24 hours · Sarojini Naidu Gallery · ₹150 per ticket · five-figure prize pool.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:34px"><button class="btn huge" data-open>REGISTER</button></div>
</section>
</main>

